  "problem": "Expressed as a whole number, $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 4=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(1\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{1}=\\frac{5\\times 4}{4\\times 1}=\\frac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{4}=\\frac{20÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{5}{1}=5\n$$",
